A||N||7||FieroFanaticus2||HUMMING from Brakes!?!?!?||||5||
Z||000000||FieroFanaticus2||08-02-2001||05:41 PM||||Ok, so I was naughty and drove my car really fast on some back country roads where there were no little kittens or people to run over.<BR>I was doing around 90 or so and hitting the brakes hard to make it around sharp turns.<BR>When I felt like being a normal citizen again I drove normally to a local gas station.  When I got out of my car I smelled something burning, looked down and noticed by rear brakes were smoking!  I waited until the smoking stopped before filling up with gas, just in case.  Everything seemed fine for a couple of days, but now when I use the brakes it sometimes lets out a loud 'hum' from the front drivers side.  Sounds like something is viberating and i can feel it through the pedal.  What is causing this?  I'm thinking maybe a loose brake pad or caliper?  Is my 1/2 hour of thrill riding going to cost me a fortune?  Any ideas please let me know, thanks.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dump_1.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fast85GT||08-02-2001||06:01 PM||||Well, how new were they? Look at them closely.  Do they have little cracks everywhere.  That would be heat-checking.  Maybe you have glazed you brake pads.  If you are in the habit of driving like a sports car should be driven, not like a little grandma in a corvette, you should look into vented brakes.<P>------------------<BR>Jeremy H<BR>Black 1985 Pontiac Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroFanaticus2||08-02-2001||06:20 PM||||I had the Pads and rotors replaced on all fours just a couple of months ago, and the calipers on all EXCEPT the one that is humming were also replaced at that time.  Since I had a shop do it for me, I am assumming they used rebuilt parts that were the same as the originals.  All together, including 2 new rear tires, it cost me nearly $1200.  Is there someone in Wisconsin or near the wisconsin border that would be willing to re-do the drivers side brake for me and look over the others to see if they are done correctly?  I'm starting to think that the shop I took it to is not familiar with Fiero's.  I would be willing to pay a reasonable price for a mechanic who is knowledgable with FIERO'S to fix my problem.<BR>Just let me know!!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dump_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-02-2001||07:36 PM||||Welcome to the land of abused brakes....<P>you warped or worse cracked a rotor.<P>Instead of driving gently while they were way hot, you parked. the area inside the caliper cooled at a diferant rate than the rest.<P>If you are real lucky it can be machined out. Don't hold your breath.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fast85GT||08-02-2001||07:57 PM||||Good idea.  You can usually tell when a mechanic is not familiar with Fieros.  If you had one of the large torx bolts back out, it could make some bad noise.<P>------------------<BR>Jeremy H<BR>Black 1985 Pontiac Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||starfighter007@msn.com||08-02-2001||09:41 PM||||are you sure this is a humming noise or a grinding noise? and why dont you take the wheel of your self and have looksie.maybe your pads are wore out.||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroFanaticus2||08-02-2001||10:16 PM||||No, not grinding, HIGH PITCH HUMMING.  The pads should not be worn out, they are only a couple of months old and i have only driven the car about 600 miles since they were put on (I only drive it about 3000 a year, if that).  Where can I get a set of vented brakes (rotors?) at a reasonable price?  The FieroStore is rather expensive when it comes to much of their products.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dump_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Wolfhound||08-02-2001||10:28 PM||||Grab the top of your Right front wheel and pull and push in and out. If you feel movement you need to repack and tighten your wheel bearings. My guestament anyway.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
